# Tree-level predictions: one row per published tree, carrying what a model
# says about the tree rather than what a source recorded. Built by the
# `refresh-predictions` [[cloud.job]] in ../trilogy.toml, daily, in the same
# tick as the rest of the core and ordered after the two jobs it consumes.
#
# It predicts crown width and height, from a measured diameter where the
# source recorded one and otherwise from a diameter predicted from the tree's
# age, and reserves `predicted_age_years`, published as null until a model
# exists. The map reads both predictions, through the browser projection, as
# the size of a tree's sprite.
#
# The age and height models are fitted here, on every build, from the
# published trees (see "The fits" below), and the fits are published beside
# the predictions. The crown model is fitted offline on Tallo, a static
# reference dataset, and read from a CSV.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The crown model, and where it comes from
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#     crown_width_m = 2 * scale * dbh_cm ** b        (dbh clamped to the fitted range)
#
# A genus-level power law fitted on Tallo (Jucker et al. 2022, CC-BY 4.0;
# 312,829 trees with a measured crown radius across 1,453 genera), which is
# the only open dataset that supports a fit at genus rank -- the published
# urban crown models are species-level and cover a few dozen species between
# them. `crown_allometry_fit.py` does the fit and writes
# `crown_width_coefficients.csv`; its docstring carries the method, the
# quality gate, the fallback order (genus -> family -> division) and the two
# things the model deliberately is not: calibrated to open-grown urban trees
# (Tallo is forest plots, and runs 15-30% low against the one urban reference
# we have), and adjusted for stand density (Bechtold 2003 tried a basal-area
# term over 87 species and dropped it as unstable). Both are curation
# questions this dataset exists to put numbers in front of.
#
# The coefficient table is a CSV `file` datasource, joined on `genus`. The
# genus comes from the enrichment table rather than from the first word of
# `species`, because the enrichment table has already corrected the
# misspellings a city publishes (`Petula pendula` -> Betula) and it is also
# what supplies `tree_form`, which decides the fallback for a genus the table
# does not carry: a conifer takes the gymnosperm fit, a palm takes none (a
# palm's crown does not scale with its stem, and Tallo has no palm crowns),
# anything else the angiosperm fit. The sentinels -- `Unknown`, `Dead`,
# `Shrub` -- take the all-Tallo fit, since their division is unknown too.
#
# The CSV's division and global rows are the fallbacks, read as data and
# keyed by the tree's fallback class. `crown_model_level` says which of those
# a row got, and `crown_model_n` how many Tallo trees stood behind it, so a
# curator can filter to the rows a real genus fit produced.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The age fallback: a diameter for a tree that has a planting date and no DBH
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# For a tree with a planting date and no diameter, `predicted_dbh_cm` stands
# in as the crown model's input, and `crown_dbh_source` says so. The fit is
# below, under "Diameter from age".
#
# `predicted_dbh_cm` is published for every tree with an age, not only the
# ones that need it, so the trees with a measured diameter are the check on
# the fit. `age_years` is observed -- planting date to the day
# the parquet was built -- and `predicted_age_years` stays reserved for a
# model that infers age where no date was recorded.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stand density: carried, not applied
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `local_tree_density_per_ha` counts published trees in a 50 m grid cell
# around each tree (0.25 ha, so trees per hectare is the count times four).
# The grid is in degrees, with the longitude step scaled by the cosine of the
# cell's own latitude band -- a Taylor polynomial, since the planner has no
# `cos` -- so a cell is 50 m on both axes anywhere the map goes. It is a
# covariate for the curation stage to plot crown width against, and it does
# not enter the prediction; see the fit script for why no sourced model has
# a usable density term.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why this job reads sources and never models
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The two inputs are reached through `full_tree_info_source.preql` and
# `tree_enrichment_source.preql`: root datasources over the published
# parquets, named the same as the managed datasources that write them, which
# is what lets trilogy-cloud order this job after `publish-full` and
# `refresh-enrichment` in one tick. Importing the models themselves would make
# this job adopt their targets -- rebuild the rollup, and run the enrichment
# LLM -- inside its own container. `test_the_core_reads_only_published_parquets`
# and `test_the_prediction_job_consumes_its_inputs_by_name` pin both.
#
# Neither this file nor the two source files is in the frontend's model
# bundle, and this one must not be: it declares `genus` as a key where the
# enrichment model the browser plans against has it as a property.

import full_tree_info_source;
import tree_enrichment_source;
import tree_height_source;

# --- Inputs' publication time ---------------------------------------------

property <*>.prediction_inputs_published_through datetime; # newer of the rollup's and the enrichment table's Last-Modified

root datasource prediction_inputs_publication (
    data_updated_through: prediction_inputs_published_through
)
file `./prediction_inputs_probe.py`;

# --- Crown coefficients, one row per genus Tallo knows --------------------

property genus.crown_coefficient_level string; # the CSV holds family / division / global rows too, for the record; only genus rows are read
property genus.crown_fit_level string; # genus | family | division: which fit this genus's row carries
property genus.crown_fit_taxon string; # the taxon that fit was made on
property genus.crown_fit_n int; # Tallo trees behind the fit
property genus.crown_fit_b float; # exponent on dbh_cm
property genus.crown_fit_scale float; # exp(ln_a + sigma^2/2): crown_radius_m = scale * dbh_cm ** b
property genus.crown_fit_dbh_max_cm float; # largest stem the fit saw; predictions clamp to it

root datasource crown_width_coefficients (
    level: crown_coefficient_level,
    taxon: genus,
    fit_level: crown_fit_level,
    fit_taxon: crown_fit_taxon,
    n: crown_fit_n,
    b: crown_fit_b,
    scale: crown_fit_scale,
    dbh_max_cm: crown_fit_dbh_max_cm,
)
grain (genus)
file `./crown_width_coefficients.csv`
where crown_coefficient_level = 'genus';

# The class a tree falls back to when its genus has no fit, decided by the
# enrichment table's growth form, and named as the fallback rows name it.
# Shared by every model: a palm's crown does not scale with its stem, its
# stem does not thicken with age, and its height does neither.
auto crown_fallback_class <- case
    when species = 'Unknown' or species = 'Dead' or species = 'Shrub' then 'all'
    when tree_form = 'palm' or species = 'Palm' or species = 'Cactus' then 'none'
    when tree_form = 'conifer' then 'Gymnosperm'
    else 'Angiosperm'
end;

# The same CSV's division and global rows: the fit a tree whose genus has no
# row falls back to, picked by the tree's fallback class.
key crown_fallback_taxon string; # Angiosperm | Gymnosperm | all
property crown_fallback_taxon.crown_fallback_row_level string; # division | global
property crown_fallback_taxon.crown_fallback_row_n int;
property crown_fallback_taxon.crown_fallback_row_b float;
property crown_fallback_taxon.crown_fallback_row_scale float;
property crown_fallback_taxon.crown_fallback_row_dbh_max_cm float;

root datasource crown_width_fallbacks (
    level: crown_fallback_row_level,
    taxon: crown_fallback_taxon,
    n: crown_fallback_row_n,
    b: crown_fallback_row_b,
    scale: crown_fallback_row_scale,
    dbh_max_cm: crown_fallback_row_dbh_max_cm,
)
grain (crown_fallback_taxon)
file `./crown_width_coefficients.csv`
where crown_fallback_row_level = 'division' or crown_fallback_row_level = 'global';

def crown_fallback_row(value, taxon) -> max(value ? crown_fallback_taxon = taxon) by *;
def crown_fallback(value) -> case
    when crown_fallback_class = 'Angiosperm' then @crown_fallback_row(value, 'Angiosperm')
    when crown_fallback_class = 'Gymnosperm' then @crown_fallback_row(value, 'Gymnosperm')
    when crown_fallback_class = 'all' then @crown_fallback_row(value, 'all')
    else null
end;
auto crown_fallback_level <- @crown_fallback(crown_fallback_row_level);
auto crown_fallback_n <- @crown_fallback(crown_fallback_row_n);
auto crown_fallback_b <- @crown_fallback(crown_fallback_row_b);
auto crown_fallback_scale <- @crown_fallback(crown_fallback_row_scale);
auto crown_fallback_dbh_max_cm <- @crown_fallback(crown_fallback_row_dbh_max_cm);

# --- The tree's own measurements ------------------------------------------

const dbh_cm_per_inch <- 2.54;
const days_per_year <- 365.25;

# A non-positive diameter is a placeholder, not a stem.
auto dbh_cm <- case when diameter_at_breast_height > 0 then diameter_at_breast_height * dbh_cm_per_inch else null end;

# Planting date to the day the parquet is built. The ingest publishes a
# planting date only when it is one -- `enforce_tree_schema` nulls a date in
# the future or before 1500, and a city's own ingest nulls the default its
# portal stamps (Edmonton's 1990-06-01, Melbourne's 1900-01-01) -- so there is
# nothing to second-guess here.
auto age_years <- case when plant_date is null then null else date_diff(plant_date, current_date(), day) / days_per_year end;


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The fits
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The diameter-from-age and height models are fitted here, on every build,
# on the published trees this job reads, rather than offline into a CSV:
#
#     log10(y) = a + b * log10(x)
#
# ordinary least squares from the group's moments, once per genus, once per
# fallback class and once over every usable tree. The predictions apply the
# power law the fit implies, back-transform bias corrected (Baskerville 1972):
#
#     y = scale * x ** b,    scale = 10 ** (a + sigma^2 * ln(10) / 2)
#
# with sigma the residual standard deviation in log10 units. (`log` takes an
# integer base, so the fit is in log10; the slope is the same in any base.)
#
# A genus's own fit is applied when it passes its model's gate; otherwise
# the tree's fallback class's fit, when that passes; otherwise the fit over
# every usable tree. The fits are published (`allometry_genus_fits`,
# `allometry_fallback_fits`, at the end of this file) for curation.

const ln_10 <- 2.302585092994046;
const fit_max_dbh_cm <- 508.0; # 200 inches, shared.ingest.DBH_MAX_INCHES: past it a diameter is a typo

def fit_n(x, grp) -> count(x) by grp;
def fit_b(x, y, grp) -> ((avg(x * y) by grp) - (avg(x) by grp) * (avg(y) by grp)) / ((avg(x * x) by grp) - (avg(x) by grp) ** 2);
def fit_a(x, y, b, grp) -> (avg(y) by grp) - b * (avg(x) by grp);
def fit_s(x, y, b, n, grp) -> case when n > 2 then sqrt(greatest(n / (n - 2) * ((avg(y * y) by grp) - (avg(y) by grp) ** 2 - b * ((avg(x * y) by grp) - (avg(x) by grp) * (avg(y) by grp))), 0.0)) else null end;
def fit_r2(x, y, b, grp) -> b * ((avg(x * y) by grp) - (avg(x) by grp) * (avg(y) by grp)) / ((avg(y * y) by grp) - (avg(y) by grp) ** 2);
def fit_xmax(x, grp) -> max(x) by grp;
# The same over every usable tree: `by *` cannot be passed as an argument.
def fit_n_all(x) -> count(x) by *;
def fit_b_all(x, y) -> ((avg(x * y) by *) - (avg(x) by *) * (avg(y) by *)) / ((avg(x * x) by *) - (avg(x) by *) ** 2);
def fit_a_all(x, y, b) -> (avg(y) by *) - b * (avg(x) by *);
def fit_s_all(x, y, b, n) -> case when n > 2 then sqrt(greatest(n / (n - 2) * ((avg(y * y) by *) - (avg(y) by *) ** 2 - b * ((avg(x * y) by *) - (avg(x) by *) * (avg(y) by *))), 0.0)) else null end;
def fit_r2_all(x, y, b) -> b * ((avg(x * y) by *) - (avg(x) by *) * (avg(y) by *)) / ((avg(y * y) by *) - (avg(y) by *) ** 2);
def fit_xmax_all(x) -> max(x) by *;

def fit_scale(a, s) -> 10 ** (a + s ** 2 * ln_10 / 2);

# A fallback class takes its own fit when that passes the gate, else the fit
# over every tree; a palm takes none.
def fallback_level(passes) -> case
    when crown_fallback_class = 'none' then null
    when crown_fallback_class != 'all' and passes then 'division'
    else 'global'
end;
def pick_fallback(level, division, everything) -> case when level = 'division' then division when level = 'global' then everything else null end;

# --- Diameter from age --------------------------------------------------------
#
#     dbh_cm = scale * age_years ** b        (age clamped to [1, the fit's oldest tree])
#
# For a tree with a planting date and no diameter (303,612 in September 2026,
# most of them Amsterdam's), so the crown and height models have a stem to
# apply to. Fitted on every published tree carrying both a planting date and
# a measured diameter (1.9M in 23 cities in September 2026). No open
# reference records age and diameter for urban trees at genus rank -- Tallo
# has no ages, and the Urban Tree Database is ~170 US species by climate zone
# -- and the population this serves is the rollup's own.
#
# The window is 1-200 years: under a year the power law heads for zero, and
# past 200 the rollup holds a few dozen estimated ages on monumental Berlin
# and Paris trees, which should not set a street tree's clamp. The ingests
# null the planting dates that are not ages (a portal's stamped default,
# dates before 1500 or in the future), so nothing here second-guesses them.
#
# The gate: n >= 100, r2 >= 0.2 (Lagerstroemia's r2 is 0.00), 0.3 <= b <= 1.3,
# and an oldest fitted tree of 20 years, since a fit on saplings alone would
# clamp every mature tree to a sapling's stem.

const dbh_fit_min_n <- 100;
const dbh_fit_min_r2 <- 0.2;
const dbh_fit_min_b <- 0.3;
const dbh_fit_max_b <- 1.3;
const dbh_fit_min_age_years <- 1.0;
const dbh_fit_max_age_years <- 200.0;
const dbh_fit_min_oldest_years <- 20.0;

def dbh_fit_passes(n, b, r2, xmax) -> case
    when n >= dbh_fit_min_n and r2 >= dbh_fit_min_r2 and b >= dbh_fit_min_b and b <= dbh_fit_max_b
        and xmax >= log(dbh_fit_min_oldest_years, 10) then true
    else false
end;

auto dbh_fit_tree <- case
    when age_years >= dbh_fit_min_age_years and age_years <= dbh_fit_max_age_years
        and dbh_cm <= fit_max_dbh_cm and crown_fallback_class != 'none' then true
    else false
end;
auto dbh_fit_x <- case when dbh_fit_tree then log(age_years, 10) else null end;
auto dbh_fit_y <- case when dbh_fit_tree then log(dbh_cm, 10) else null end;

auto dbh_genus_fit_n <- @fit_n(dbh_fit_x, genus);
auto dbh_genus_fit_b <- @fit_b(dbh_fit_x, dbh_fit_y, genus);
auto dbh_genus_fit_a <- @fit_a(dbh_fit_x, dbh_fit_y, dbh_genus_fit_b, genus);
auto dbh_genus_fit_sigma <- @fit_s(dbh_fit_x, dbh_fit_y, dbh_genus_fit_b, dbh_genus_fit_n, genus);
auto dbh_genus_fit_r2 <- @fit_r2(dbh_fit_x, dbh_fit_y, dbh_genus_fit_b, genus);
auto dbh_genus_fit_age_max_years <- 10 ** @fit_xmax(dbh_fit_x, genus);
auto dbh_genus_fit_scale <- @fit_scale(dbh_genus_fit_a, dbh_genus_fit_sigma);
auto dbh_genus_fit_passes <- case when genus is null then false else @dbh_fit_passes(dbh_genus_fit_n, dbh_genus_fit_b, dbh_genus_fit_r2, @fit_xmax(dbh_fit_x, genus)) end;

auto dbh_division_fit_n <- @fit_n(dbh_fit_x, crown_fallback_class);
auto dbh_division_fit_b <- @fit_b(dbh_fit_x, dbh_fit_y, crown_fallback_class);
auto dbh_division_fit_a <- @fit_a(dbh_fit_x, dbh_fit_y, dbh_division_fit_b, crown_fallback_class);
auto dbh_division_fit_sigma <- @fit_s(dbh_fit_x, dbh_fit_y, dbh_division_fit_b, dbh_division_fit_n, crown_fallback_class);
auto dbh_division_fit_r2 <- @fit_r2(dbh_fit_x, dbh_fit_y, dbh_division_fit_b, crown_fallback_class);
auto dbh_division_fit_passes <- @dbh_fit_passes(dbh_division_fit_n, dbh_division_fit_b, dbh_division_fit_r2, @fit_xmax(dbh_fit_x, crown_fallback_class));

auto dbh_global_fit_n <- @fit_n_all(dbh_fit_x);
auto dbh_global_fit_b <- @fit_b_all(dbh_fit_x, dbh_fit_y);
auto dbh_global_fit_a <- @fit_a_all(dbh_fit_x, dbh_fit_y, dbh_global_fit_b);
auto dbh_global_fit_sigma <- @fit_s_all(dbh_fit_x, dbh_fit_y, dbh_global_fit_b, dbh_global_fit_n);
auto dbh_global_fit_r2 <- @fit_r2_all(dbh_fit_x, dbh_fit_y, dbh_global_fit_b);
auto dbh_global_fit_passes <- @dbh_fit_passes(dbh_global_fit_n, dbh_global_fit_b, dbh_global_fit_r2, @fit_xmax_all(dbh_fit_x));

auto dbh_fallback_level <- @fallback_level(dbh_division_fit_passes);
auto dbh_fallback_n <- @pick_fallback(dbh_fallback_level, dbh_division_fit_n, dbh_global_fit_n);
auto dbh_fallback_b <- @pick_fallback(dbh_fallback_level, dbh_division_fit_b, dbh_global_fit_b);
auto dbh_fallback_r2 <- @pick_fallback(dbh_fallback_level, dbh_division_fit_r2, dbh_global_fit_r2);
auto dbh_fallback_scale <- @pick_fallback(dbh_fallback_level,
    @fit_scale(dbh_division_fit_a, dbh_division_fit_sigma), @fit_scale(dbh_global_fit_a, dbh_global_fit_sigma));
auto dbh_fallback_age_max_years <- @pick_fallback(dbh_fallback_level,
    10 ** @fit_xmax(dbh_fit_x, crown_fallback_class), 10 ** @fit_xmax_all(dbh_fit_x));

auto dbh_scale <- case when dbh_genus_fit_passes then dbh_genus_fit_scale else dbh_fallback_scale end;
auto dbh_b <- case when dbh_genus_fit_passes then dbh_genus_fit_b else dbh_fallback_b end;
auto dbh_age_max_years <- case when dbh_genus_fit_passes then dbh_genus_fit_age_max_years else dbh_fallback_age_max_years end;

# Clamped to [1 year, the fit's oldest tree], with the null case spelled out
# for the reason the crown clamp spells it out.
auto dbh_age_years <- case
    when age_years is null then null
    else least(greatest(age_years, 1.0), dbh_age_max_years)
end;

auto predicted_dbh_cm <- dbh_scale * (dbh_age_years ** dbh_b);

auto dbh_model_level <- case
    when age_years is null then 'none'
    when dbh_genus_fit_passes then 'genus'
    when dbh_fallback_level is null then 'none'
    else dbh_fallback_level
end;
auto dbh_model_taxon <- case
    when dbh_model_level = 'none' then null
    when dbh_model_level = 'genus' then genus
    when dbh_model_level = 'global' then 'all'
    else crown_fallback_class
end;
auto dbh_model_n <- case
    when dbh_model_level = 'none' then null
    when dbh_model_level = 'genus' then dbh_genus_fit_n
    else dbh_fallback_n
end;

# --- Crown width -----------------------------------------------------------

# What the crown model is applied to: the measured diameter, else the one
# predicted from age. `crown_dbh_source` records which.
auto crown_input_dbh_cm <- coalesce(dbh_cm, predicted_dbh_cm);
auto crown_dbh_source <- case
    when dbh_cm is not null then 'measured'
    when predicted_dbh_cm is not null then 'age'
    else null
end;

auto crown_scale <- coalesce(crown_fit_scale, crown_fallback_scale);
auto crown_b <- coalesce(crown_fit_b, crown_fallback_b);
auto crown_dbh_max_cm <- coalesce(crown_fit_dbh_max_cm, crown_fallback_dbh_max_cm);

# Clamped to [1 cm, the fit's largest stem]: an exponent below one keeps a
# power law tame, but a data-entry DBH of 400 inches should predict the crown
# of the largest tree the genus was fitted on, not extrapolate past it. The
# null case is spelled out because DuckDB's greatest() skips nulls -- without
# it a tree with no diameter is a 1 cm stem with a 70 cm crown.
def crown_width_at(dbh) -> case
    when dbh is null then null
    else 2 * crown_scale * (least(greatest(dbh, 1.0), crown_dbh_max_cm) ** crown_b)
end;

auto predicted_crown_width_m <- @crown_width_at(crown_input_dbh_cm);
# The crown the measured stem alone predicts: what the height gates compare a
# survey's crown with, independent of the age model.
auto measured_stem_crown_width_m <- @crown_width_at(dbh_cm);

# The observed crown, where a source recorded one (tree_common's crown_width_m:
# Tokyo's survey, an OSM diameter_crown tag, a reviewed satellite crown), is
# published beside the prediction rather than folded into it, so curation can
# plot one against the other and the prediction stays a prediction.

auto crown_model_level <- case
    when crown_input_dbh_cm is null then 'none'
    when crown_fit_scale is not null then crown_fit_level
    when crown_fallback_class = 'none' then 'none'
    else crown_fallback_level
end;
auto crown_model_taxon <- case
    when crown_model_level = 'none' then null
    when crown_fit_scale is not null then crown_fit_taxon
    else crown_fallback_class
end;
auto crown_model_n <- case
    when crown_model_level = 'none' then null
    when crown_fit_scale is not null then crown_fit_n
    else crown_fallback_n
end;

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The height model
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#     height_m = scale * dbh_cm ** b        (dbh clamped to [1, the fit's largest stem])
#
# Fitted on the map's own trees that carry a LIDAR height (tree_height.preql;
# Boston, Cambridge and the Arnold Arboretum today), not on Tallo: Tallo's
# forest-grown heights missed Cambridge's street trees by a median 6.2 m
# where a LIDAR fit missed by 2.8 m (docs/TREE_HEIGHT.md). It is one metro's
# street trees until another city's survey is added to tree_height_source.
#
# Same input diameter as the crown, same fallbacks by growth form, same
# clamp. The observed height, where a city published one, is carried beside
# the prediction as `height_m` (tree_height_source.preql) and checked
# against it below.
#
# The gate: n >= 100, 0.01 <= b <= 1.0, and a largest fitted stem of at
# least 30 cm. No r2 floor, unlike the age fit: a flat fit is a true answer
# for a genus that stays small whatever its stem (Malus: 9-11 m from 10 to
# 60 cm), and an r2 floor sent it to the angiosperm fit, which calls a 30 cm
# crabapple 13 m.
#
# --- The gates on an observed height ---------------------------------------
#
# A LIDAR height is the height of the crown over the tree's point, and two
# things put the wrong crown there (docs/TREE_HEIGHT.md, "The gates"):
#
#   shaded       the crown is far wider than the tree's own stem could carry:
#                a sapling or an understorey tree under a neighbour's canopy,
#                lent the neighbour's height. Wider than `shaded_crown_ratio`
#                times the radius the crown model predicts from the stem.
#                Inventory trees past that ratio read 40% tall on average;
#                below twice it, not at all.
#   out_of_band  the height is outside `height_band_sigmas` standard
#                deviations of the height fit's residual for the tree's
#                genus, either way: a geocode on a building edge, or on a
#                shrub beside a mature tree. The band is the fit's own
#                spread, so a genus that varies more is given more room.
#
# Both compare the survey with a prediction from the stem, so both need a
# stem someone measured. Imagery predictions and reviewed satellite
# detections carry a diameter a model supplied from the same crown, and a
# tree with no diameter has only its genus median to be compared with: those
# keep their survey height, `unchecked`. A shaded or out-of-band tree falls
# back to its predicted height on the map; `height_m` keeps what the survey
# said, for curation.
#
# The fit leaves out the trees the gates refuse, so a neighbour's crown does
# not teach a sapling a mature height: shaded trees before fitting, then the
# out-of-band ones, refitting twice (rounds 0, 1, 2 below) so the outliers do
# not widen the band that judges them. Fitting to convergence took nine
# rounds on Boston in November 2026 and moved the band 1% past round 2.

const shaded_crown_ratio <- 3.0;
const height_band_sigmas <- 3.0;
const height_fit_min_n <- 100;
const height_fit_min_b <- 0.01;
const height_fit_max_b <- 1.0;
const height_fit_min_dbh_cm <- 2.5; # one inch: below it a "diameter" is a placeholder
const height_fit_min_largest_dbh_cm <- 30.0;

def height_fit_passes(n, b, xmax) -> case
    when n >= height_fit_min_n and b >= height_fit_min_b and b <= height_fit_max_b
        and xmax >= log(height_fit_min_largest_dbh_cm, 10) then true
    else false
end;

# Within the band of a round's fit: the residual, with the diameter clamped
# as the predictions clamp it, inside height_band_sigmas sigma.
def height_in_band(x, y, a, b, s, xmax) -> case
    when abs(y - a - b * least(greatest(x, 0.0), xmax)) <= height_band_sigmas * s then true
    else false
end;

auto height_stem_measured <- case
    when crown_dbh_source != 'measured' or crown_dbh_source is null then false
    when data_source like 'PREDICTED_%' or data_source like 'SATELLITE_%' then false
    else true
end;

auto height_shaded <- case
    when height_crown_radius_m > shaded_crown_ratio * measured_stem_crown_width_m / 2 then true
    else false
end;

auto height_fit_tree <- case
    when height_m > 0 and height_stem_measured and not height_shaded
        and dbh_cm >= height_fit_min_dbh_cm and dbh_cm <= fit_max_dbh_cm
        and crown_fallback_class != 'none' then true
    else false
end;

# Round 0: every unshaded tree.
auto height_r0_x <- case when height_fit_tree then log(dbh_cm, 10) else null end;
auto height_r0_y <- case when height_fit_tree then log(height_m, 10) else null end;

auto height_r0_genus_n <- @fit_n(height_r0_x, genus);
auto height_r0_genus_b <- @fit_b(height_r0_x, height_r0_y, genus);
auto height_r0_genus_a <- @fit_a(height_r0_x, height_r0_y, height_r0_genus_b, genus);
auto height_r0_genus_s <- @fit_s(height_r0_x, height_r0_y, height_r0_genus_b, height_r0_genus_n, genus);
auto height_r0_genus_xmax <- @fit_xmax(height_r0_x, genus);
auto height_r0_genus_passes <- case when genus is null then false else @height_fit_passes(height_r0_genus_n, height_r0_genus_b, height_r0_genus_xmax) end;
auto height_r0_division_n <- @fit_n(height_r0_x, crown_fallback_class);
auto height_r0_division_b <- @fit_b(height_r0_x, height_r0_y, crown_fallback_class);
auto height_r0_division_a <- @fit_a(height_r0_x, height_r0_y, height_r0_division_b, crown_fallback_class);
auto height_r0_division_s <- @fit_s(height_r0_x, height_r0_y, height_r0_division_b, height_r0_division_n, crown_fallback_class);
auto height_r0_division_xmax <- @fit_xmax(height_r0_x, crown_fallback_class);
auto height_r0_global_n <- @fit_n_all(height_r0_x);
auto height_r0_global_b <- @fit_b_all(height_r0_x, height_r0_y);
auto height_r0_global_a <- @fit_a_all(height_r0_x, height_r0_y, height_r0_global_b);
auto height_r0_global_s <- @fit_s_all(height_r0_x, height_r0_y, height_r0_global_b, height_r0_global_n);
auto height_r0_global_xmax <- @fit_xmax_all(height_r0_x);
auto height_r0_fallback_level <- @fallback_level(@height_fit_passes(height_r0_division_n, height_r0_division_b, height_r0_division_xmax));
auto height_r0_a <- case when height_r0_genus_passes then height_r0_genus_a
    else @pick_fallback(height_r0_fallback_level, height_r0_division_a, height_r0_global_a) end;
auto height_r0_b <- case when height_r0_genus_passes then height_r0_genus_b
    else @pick_fallback(height_r0_fallback_level, height_r0_division_b, height_r0_global_b) end;
auto height_r0_s <- case when height_r0_genus_passes then height_r0_genus_s
    else @pick_fallback(height_r0_fallback_level, height_r0_division_s, height_r0_global_s) end;
auto height_r0_xmax <- case when height_r0_genus_passes then height_r0_genus_xmax
    else @pick_fallback(height_r0_fallback_level, height_r0_division_xmax, height_r0_global_xmax) end;
auto height_r0_in_band <- @height_in_band(height_r0_x, height_r0_y, height_r0_a, height_r0_b, height_r0_s, height_r0_xmax);

# Round 1: the trees inside round 0's band.
auto height_r1_x <- case when height_r0_in_band then height_r0_x else null end;
auto height_r1_y <- case when height_r0_in_band then height_r0_y else null end;

auto height_r1_genus_n <- @fit_n(height_r1_x, genus);
auto height_r1_genus_b <- @fit_b(height_r1_x, height_r1_y, genus);
auto height_r1_genus_a <- @fit_a(height_r1_x, height_r1_y, height_r1_genus_b, genus);
auto height_r1_genus_s <- @fit_s(height_r1_x, height_r1_y, height_r1_genus_b, height_r1_genus_n, genus);
auto height_r1_genus_xmax <- @fit_xmax(height_r1_x, genus);
auto height_r1_genus_passes <- case when genus is null then false else @height_fit_passes(height_r1_genus_n, height_r1_genus_b, height_r1_genus_xmax) end;
auto height_r1_division_n <- @fit_n(height_r1_x, crown_fallback_class);
auto height_r1_division_b <- @fit_b(height_r1_x, height_r1_y, crown_fallback_class);
auto height_r1_division_a <- @fit_a(height_r1_x, height_r1_y, height_r1_division_b, crown_fallback_class);
auto height_r1_division_s <- @fit_s(height_r1_x, height_r1_y, height_r1_division_b, height_r1_division_n, crown_fallback_class);
auto height_r1_division_xmax <- @fit_xmax(height_r1_x, crown_fallback_class);
auto height_r1_global_n <- @fit_n_all(height_r1_x);
auto height_r1_global_b <- @fit_b_all(height_r1_x, height_r1_y);
auto height_r1_global_a <- @fit_a_all(height_r1_x, height_r1_y, height_r1_global_b);
auto height_r1_global_s <- @fit_s_all(height_r1_x, height_r1_y, height_r1_global_b, height_r1_global_n);
auto height_r1_global_xmax <- @fit_xmax_all(height_r1_x);
auto height_r1_fallback_level <- @fallback_level(@height_fit_passes(height_r1_division_n, height_r1_division_b, height_r1_division_xmax));
auto height_r1_a <- case when height_r1_genus_passes then height_r1_genus_a
    else @pick_fallback(height_r1_fallback_level, height_r1_division_a, height_r1_global_a) end;
auto height_r1_b <- case when height_r1_genus_passes then height_r1_genus_b
    else @pick_fallback(height_r1_fallback_level, height_r1_division_b, height_r1_global_b) end;
auto height_r1_s <- case when height_r1_genus_passes then height_r1_genus_s
    else @pick_fallback(height_r1_fallback_level, height_r1_division_s, height_r1_global_s) end;
auto height_r1_xmax <- case when height_r1_genus_passes then height_r1_genus_xmax
    else @pick_fallback(height_r1_fallback_level, height_r1_division_xmax, height_r1_global_xmax) end;
auto height_r1_in_band <- @height_in_band(height_r1_x, height_r1_y, height_r1_a, height_r1_b, height_r1_s, height_r1_xmax);

# Round 2, the fit the predictions apply: the trees inside round 1's band.
auto height_fit_x <- case when height_r1_in_band then height_r1_x else null end;
auto height_fit_y <- case when height_r1_in_band then height_r1_y else null end;

auto height_genus_fit_n <- @fit_n(height_fit_x, genus);
auto height_genus_fit_b <- @fit_b(height_fit_x, height_fit_y, genus);
auto height_genus_fit_a <- @fit_a(height_fit_x, height_fit_y, height_genus_fit_b, genus);
auto height_genus_fit_sigma <- @fit_s(height_fit_x, height_fit_y, height_genus_fit_b, height_genus_fit_n, genus);
auto height_genus_fit_r2 <- @fit_r2(height_fit_x, height_fit_y, height_genus_fit_b, genus);
auto height_genus_fit_dbh_max_cm <- 10 ** @fit_xmax(height_fit_x, genus);
auto height_genus_fit_scale <- @fit_scale(height_genus_fit_a, height_genus_fit_sigma);
auto height_genus_fit_passes <- case when genus is null then false else @height_fit_passes(height_genus_fit_n, height_genus_fit_b, @fit_xmax(height_fit_x, genus)) end;

auto height_division_fit_n <- @fit_n(height_fit_x, crown_fallback_class);
auto height_division_fit_b <- @fit_b(height_fit_x, height_fit_y, crown_fallback_class);
auto height_division_fit_a <- @fit_a(height_fit_x, height_fit_y, height_division_fit_b, crown_fallback_class);
auto height_division_fit_sigma <- @fit_s(height_fit_x, height_fit_y, height_division_fit_b, height_division_fit_n, crown_fallback_class);
auto height_division_fit_r2 <- @fit_r2(height_fit_x, height_fit_y, height_division_fit_b, crown_fallback_class);
auto height_division_fit_passes <- @height_fit_passes(height_division_fit_n, height_division_fit_b, @fit_xmax(height_fit_x, crown_fallback_class));

auto height_global_fit_n <- @fit_n_all(height_fit_x);
auto height_global_fit_b <- @fit_b_all(height_fit_x, height_fit_y);
auto height_global_fit_a <- @fit_a_all(height_fit_x, height_fit_y, height_global_fit_b);
auto height_global_fit_sigma <- @fit_s_all(height_fit_x, height_fit_y, height_global_fit_b, height_global_fit_n);
auto height_global_fit_r2 <- @fit_r2_all(height_fit_x, height_fit_y, height_global_fit_b);
auto height_global_fit_passes <- @height_fit_passes(height_global_fit_n, height_global_fit_b, @fit_xmax_all(height_fit_x));

auto height_fallback_level <- @fallback_level(height_division_fit_passes);
auto height_fallback_n <- @pick_fallback(height_fallback_level, height_division_fit_n, height_global_fit_n);
auto height_fallback_b <- @pick_fallback(height_fallback_level, height_division_fit_b, height_global_fit_b);
auto height_fallback_r2 <- @pick_fallback(height_fallback_level, height_division_fit_r2, height_global_fit_r2);
auto height_fallback_sigma <- @pick_fallback(height_fallback_level, height_division_fit_sigma, height_global_fit_sigma);
auto height_fallback_scale <- @pick_fallback(height_fallback_level,
    @fit_scale(height_division_fit_a, height_division_fit_sigma), @fit_scale(height_global_fit_a, height_global_fit_sigma));
auto height_fallback_dbh_max_cm <- @pick_fallback(height_fallback_level,
    10 ** @fit_xmax(height_fit_x, crown_fallback_class), 10 ** @fit_xmax_all(height_fit_x));

auto height_scale <- case when height_genus_fit_passes then height_genus_fit_scale else height_fallback_scale end;
auto height_b <- case when height_genus_fit_passes then height_genus_fit_b else height_fallback_b end;
auto height_sigma <- case when height_genus_fit_passes then height_genus_fit_sigma else height_fallback_sigma end;
auto height_dbh_max_cm <- case when height_genus_fit_passes then height_genus_fit_dbh_max_cm else height_fallback_dbh_max_cm end;

# The null case spelled out for the reason the crown clamp spells it out.
auto height_dbh_cm <- case
    when crown_input_dbh_cm is null then null
    else least(greatest(crown_input_dbh_cm, 1.0), height_dbh_max_cm)
end;

# A palm gets no height, as it gets no crown: its trunk does not thicken with
# its height, and no palm stands under a Boston crown to fit.
auto predicted_height_m <- cast(height_scale * (height_dbh_cm ** height_b) as float);

# The band, as multiples of the bias-corrected prediction: the residual's
# bounds, less the correction the prediction already carries.
def height_gate(sigmas, s) -> 10 ** (sigmas * s - s ** 2 * ln_10 / 2);
auto height_gate_low <- @height_gate(-1 * height_band_sigmas, height_sigma);
auto height_gate_high <- @height_gate(height_band_sigmas, height_sigma);

auto height_check <- case
    when height_m is null then null
    when not height_stem_measured or predicted_height_m is null then 'unchecked'
    when height_shaded then 'shaded'
    when height_m > predicted_height_m * height_gate_high then 'out_of_band'
    when height_m < predicted_height_m * height_gate_low then 'out_of_band'
    else 'accepted'
end;

# The height the map draws: the survey's where it passed or could not be
# checked, else nothing, and the map falls back to predicted_height_m.
auto accepted_height_m <- case
    when height_check = 'accepted' or height_check = 'unchecked' then height_m
    else null
end;

# --- Stand density covariate ----------------------------------------------

const density_cell_lat_deg <- 0.000449; # 50 m of latitude
const density_cell_area_ha <- 0.25;

auto density_lat_index <- floor(latitude / density_cell_lat_deg);
# cos(latitude) of the cell's band, so every tree in a band shares one
# longitude step and the grid stays a grid: 1 - x^2/2 + x^4/24 - x^6/720,
# within 4e-5 of cos up to 60 degrees.
auto density_band_rad <- density_lat_index * density_cell_lat_deg * 3.141592653589793 / 180;
auto density_cos_lat <- 1 - (density_band_rad ** 2) / 2 + (density_band_rad ** 4) / 24 - (density_band_rad ** 6) / 720;
auto density_lon_index <- floor(longitude * density_cos_lat / density_cell_lat_deg);
# A tree with no coordinates lands in cell -1 rather than in a null cell, so
# the join back from the per-cell count keeps its row; its density is null.
auto density_cell <- coalesce(cast(density_lat_index as bigint) * 2000000 + cast(density_lon_index as bigint), -1);

auto local_tree_density_per_ha <- case
    when density_cell = -1 then null
    else (count(tree_id) by density_cell) / density_cell_area_ha
end;

# --- Placeholder -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Published as null until a model exists. Age is observed where a city
# publishes a planting date (`age_years`, above); a *predicted* age would be
# the inverse of the diameter-from-age fit, for a tree with a diameter and no
# date, and is not modelled yet.

# Written as a case over the tree's own diameter rather than a bare
# `cast(null as float)`: pytrilogy 0.3.353 treats a constant null as having
# no source and drops it from the persist projection ("would write 10 of 12
# declared columns"), while an expression over a tree column is carried.
auto predicted_age_years <- case when dbh_cm is null then cast(null as float) else cast(null as float) end;

# --- The published parquet ------------------------------------------------
#
# `where tree_id is not null`: the enrichment source is partial, so the
# planner joins it with a FULL join, which keeps every tree (a species with
# no enrichment row yet is still predicted, on the angiosperm fallback) but
# also emits a row for every enrichment species and coefficient genus that no
# published tree carries. Those rows have no tree, and the grain says so.

datasource tree_predictions (
    tree_id,
    city,
    ?genus,
    ?dbh_cm,
    ?age_years,
    ?predicted_dbh_cm,
    dbh_model_level,
    ?dbh_model_taxon,
    ?dbh_model_n,
    ?crown_dbh_source,
    ?predicted_crown_width_m,
    ?crown_width_m,
    crown_model_level,
    ?crown_model_taxon,
    ?crown_model_n,
    ?local_tree_density_per_ha,
    ?predicted_height_m,
    ?height_m,
    ?height_crown_radius_m,
    ?height_check,
    ?accepted_height_m,
    ?predicted_age_years,
    prediction_inputs_published_through,
)
grain (tree_id)
file f`https://storage.googleapis.com/trilogy_public_models/duckdb/trees/tree_predictions_v{data_version}.parquet`:f`gcs://trilogy_public_models/duckdb/trees/tree_predictions_v{data_version}.parquet`
where tree_id is not null
freshness by prediction_inputs_published_through
# A city's rows vanishing from this build (its parquet unreadable or shed)
# fails it; see `kept_by` in core.preql.  Dropping a city on purpose takes
# one local `trilogy refresh --skip-validation tree_predictions` build.
assert @kept_by(tree_id, city, 0.75);

# --- The published fits -------------------------------------------------------
#
# What the age and height models were fitted to on this build, for curation:
# one row per genus that had any usable tree, with its own fit and whether it
# passed the gate (a genus that did not is predicted on its fallback), and
# one row per fallback class with the fit that class falls back to. The
# predictions publish which of these each tree got (`dbh_model_*`).

datasource allometry_genus_fits (
    genus,
    ?dbh_genus_fit_n,
    ?dbh_genus_fit_b,
    ?dbh_genus_fit_scale,
    ?dbh_genus_fit_sigma,
    ?dbh_genus_fit_r2,
    ?dbh_genus_fit_age_max_years,
    ?dbh_genus_fit_passes,
    ?height_genus_fit_n,
    ?height_genus_fit_b,
    ?height_genus_fit_scale,
    ?height_genus_fit_sigma,
    ?height_genus_fit_r2,
    ?height_genus_fit_dbh_max_cm,
    ?height_genus_fit_passes,
    prediction_inputs_published_through,
)
grain (genus)
file f`https://storage.googleapis.com/trilogy_public_models/duckdb/trees/allometry_genus_fits_v{data_version}.parquet`:f`gcs://trilogy_public_models/duckdb/trees/allometry_genus_fits_v{data_version}.parquet`
where genus is not null and (dbh_genus_fit_n > 0 or height_genus_fit_n > 0)
freshness by prediction_inputs_published_through
assert @kept(genus, 0.75);

datasource allometry_fallback_fits (
    crown_fallback_class,
    ?dbh_fallback_level,
    ?dbh_fallback_n,
    ?dbh_fallback_b,
    ?dbh_fallback_scale,
    ?dbh_fallback_r2,
    ?dbh_fallback_age_max_years,
    ?height_fallback_level,
    ?height_fallback_n,
    ?height_fallback_b,
    ?height_fallback_scale,
    ?height_fallback_sigma,
    ?height_fallback_r2,
    ?height_fallback_dbh_max_cm,
    prediction_inputs_published_through,
)
grain (crown_fallback_class)
file f`https://storage.googleapis.com/trilogy_public_models/duckdb/trees/allometry_fallback_fits_v{data_version}.parquet`:f`gcs://trilogy_public_models/duckdb/trees/allometry_fallback_fits_v{data_version}.parquet`
where crown_fallback_class is not null and crown_fallback_class != 'none'
freshness by prediction_inputs_published_through
assert @kept(crown_fallback_class, 0.75);
